PM01-blanket-metadata-refusal
  semantic: promisor metadata alone (extensions.partialClone, remote.*.promisor, partialclonefilter, paired .promisor+.pack) is not a refusal: a complete promisor-marked repository keeps the verdict of its full-clone twin
  expects unmet rows: ['PG01', 'PG02', 'PG03', 'PG04', 'PG05', 'PG06', 'PG09', 'PG11', 'PG12', 'PG13', 'PG15', 'PG17', 'PG19', 'PG21', 'PG22', 'PG23', 'PG24', 'PG26', 'PG28', 'PG30', 'PG31', 'PG33', 'PG35', 'PG39', 'PG41', 'PG43', 'PG44', 'PG46', 'PG47', 'PG48']
  execution: grid
PM02-blanket-unknown-refusal
  semantic: the unknown rule covers a locally unresolved citation that names unavailable referenced content; it must not cover every absent citation, or the historical errata-marked clone (absent hash corrected by errata) would stop being an ordinary failed finding. The mutant refuses every absent (or unknown) citation, before the errata ledger is consulted.
  expects unmet rows: ['PG22', 'PG23', 'PG24', 'PG25', 'PG29', 'PG30', 'PG33', 'PG34', 'PG37', 'PG41', 'PG42', 'PG46', 'PG47']
  execution: grid
PM03-skip-required-object-availability
  semantic: objects the audit actually reads must be available: a missing required commit/tree/blob/report object is could-not-evaluate (exit 2), never a clean or failed verdict. This mutant removes the store's missing-object computation (the completeness element); PM04 removes the decision that uses it. Both attack the same verdict surface, so the grid kills them alike: one of them surviving is not evidence about the other.
  expects unmet rows: ['PG13', 'PG14', 'PG15', 'PG16', 'PG17', 'PG18', 'PG19', 'PG20', 'PG21', 'PG33', 'PG34', 'PG46', 'PG47']
  execution: grid
PM04-accept-unavailable-referenced-object
  semantic: a locally unresolved citation that names unavailable referenced local content is could-not-evaluate rather than proved absent; the mutant accepts it as absent and reports an ordinary failed finding
  expects unmet rows: ['PG33', 'PG34', 'PG46', 'PG47']
  execution: grid
PM05-ignore-second-repo
  semantic: a citation missing in one audited repository but present in the other audited repository is resolvable; the mutant consults the first repository only
  expects unmet rows: ['PG26', 'PG27']
  execution: grid
PM06-swallow-actual-read-error
  semantic: a failing read of the object the audit needs is could-not-evaluate; the mutant swallows the failure and guesses a value, turning an unavailable object into a verdict
  expects unmet rows: ['PG13', 'PG14', 'PG15', 'PG16', 'PG17', 'PG18', 'PG19', 'PG20', 'PG21', 'PG28', 'PG43']
  execution: grid
PM07-full-all-missing-gate
  semantic: the store-wide missing walk starts from every present root of the store; the mutant adds --all to that root set, so a broken unselected REF target that is not among the present roots enters the diagnosis. The mutant does NOT broaden the name-hiding (opaque) walk, which stays rooted at the pinned root only. On the finite 47 this mutant may survive; that survival is an observation of this candidate, not a global-equivalence proof: the canonical mutant can also differ on a broken unselected REF target that is not among the present roots, so no universal no-op is claimed. Supplemental post-fix experiments, specially named and separate from the 47 and from this canonical operator, may test unselected-ref opacity.
  expects unmet rows: ['PG31', 'PG32', 'PG35', 'PG36', 'PG44', 'PG45', 'PG48']
  execution: grid
PM08-no-lazy-fetch-review-only
  semantic: review-only note, uncredited: the candidate's repository-reading wrappers carry --no-lazy-fetch, so a missing promisor object is not fetched through the promisor remote (transport, repository programs, signatures) on the paths those wrappers read. This is a manual reading of the candidate's isolation flags, not a proof about every git call. The element is not part of the executed inventory: no mutant is built, no grid invocation is made for it, and it is never credited with a kill
  expects unmet rows: -
  execution: review-only
PM09-peel-bare-full-hash-lookup
  semantic: the bare full-hash lookup stays unpeeled: a citation of a locally present blob resolves as found; peeling it to ^{commit} turns a present blob into a missing object
  expects unmet rows: ['PG38', 'PG39']
  execution: grid
PM10-ignore-opaque-nonleaf-missing
  semantic: when a citation is unresolved, a missing tree or commit may conceal the dependency names, so the citation is unevaluable (exit 2); the mutant ignores that hole and reports an ordinary failed finding. A known-present blob that merely fails commit peeling keeps its wrong-type finding either way.
  expects unmet rows: ['PG46', 'PG47']
  execution: grid
PM11-any-hole-refusal
  semantic: no missing-object state may be an unconditional audit precondition: an unrelated blob hole next to a fabricated citation keeps the ordinary failed finding, and an unused tree hole next to fully resolved citations keeps the clean verdict; the mutant refuses on any hole
  expects unmet rows: ['PG13', 'PG14', 'PG15', 'PG16', 'PG17', 'PG18', 'PG19', 'PG20', 'PG21', 'PG22', 'PG28', 'PG31', 'PG32', 'PG33', 'PG34', 'PG35', 'PG36', 'PG37', 'PG41', 'PG42', 'PG43', 'PG44', 'PG45', 'PG46', 'PG47', 'PG48']
  execution: grid
